  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 139 PM CDT Tue Jul 21 2026\n\nFor those looking for a brief break from the warm and humid stretch \nof weather, the next days are for you. For those who want to soak up \nas much summer as possible, we have something for you as well this \nweekend into early next week. \n\nAloft...the weather pattern is looking a bit more like early fall in \nour region with a deep upper trough digging into the eastern Great \nLakes. Northwesterly flow is advecting a cool and dry airmass in our \ndirection. At the surface high pressure will continue slowly ooze \nsouthward into the upper Midwest, with deep low pressure inching \neastward through eastern Canada. There is an extensive \ncumulus/stratocumulus field across the region and spreading \ninto NE IL. Cloud depths should remain shallow enough to prevent\nwidespread showers, however enhanced convergence hear the \nlakeshore, both on the IL and IN side should support some lake \neffect rain showers, not super common for July. The convergence \nwill be maximized in NE IL this evening, and then into NW IN \nlater tonight into Wednesday as the land breeze convergence \nkicks in. We are already seeing some fairly robust showers \nacross far northern Lake Michigan. Given that lake induced \nequilibrium levels are just high enough with +15 C of \ndifferential between the surface and 850 temperatures, we would \nnot be surprised for a waterspout or two to develop particularly\nalong any focused convergence bands this evening. Winds are \ngusting into the mid 20 kt range (near 30 mph) along and behind \nthe front, and waves will quickly build in the wake of the front\nleading to a High Swim Risk after sunset at all southern Lake \nMichigan beaches. Away from the lakeshore clouds should clear \nenough and combined with cool/dry advection later tonight, and we\nwill see temperatures fall into the 50s in the clear areas and \nfor points north of I-80 inland. \n\nHigh pressure will shift closer to the lower Great Lakes during\nthe day on Wednesday, and then overhead late Wednesday night \nand Thursday. Northerly/onshore flow will maintain the High Swim\nRisk on Wednesday before lighter winds arrive with the center \nof high pressure. Highs will also struggle to get out of the \nmid 70s. This will set the stage for the coolest night across \nthe region this summer, with widespread lows in the 50s away \nfrom urban Chicago. We could even see a few 40s along the IL/WI \nborder. With high pressure overhead or slightly shifting east on\nThursday, Thursday will feature comfortable temperatures in the\nmid to upper 70s and some afternoon cumulus clouds.\n\nA wave in northwest flow will pass by to our south along the eastern \nfringes of an expanding upper ridge/heat dome across the west and \nthe plains states on Friday, so we will get grazed with some \ncloudiness, otherwise it will be dry with temperatures getting back \nto either side of 80 (still slightly below normal given we are \nat the climatological peak of average highs). 850 mb \ntemperatures warm considerably Saturday and especially on \nSunday, where upper 80s to low (to mid) 90s are easily supported\nunder partly cloudy skies. The ridge will not be centered \noverhead, which will open the door to convective systems during \nthe latter half of the weekend into early next week. \n\nKMD",
